      "text": "To find the total surface area of the right pyramid, we need to calculate the area of the square base and the area of the four triangular faces.\n\n1. **Calculate the area of the square base:**\n\n   The side length of the square base is given as \\(10 \\, \\text{cm}\\).\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of the base} = 10 \\, \\text{cm} \\times 10 \\, \\text{cm} = 100 \\, \\text{cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the slant height of the triangular faces:**\n\n   The peak of the pyramid is 12 cm above the center of the base. The center of the base is at the midpoint of each side of the square. To find the height of the triangles, we use the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by the slant height, the height of the pyramid, and half the side length of the base.\n   \n   Let \\( l \\) be the slant height. Then:\n   \\[\n   l = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{10 \\, \\text{cm}}{2}\\right)^2 + 12 \\, \\text{cm}^2} = \\sqrt{5^2 + 12^2} = \\sqrt{25 + 144} = \\sqrt{169} = 13 \\, \\text{cm}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the area of one triangular face:**\n\n   Each triangular face has a base of 10 cm and a slant height of 13 cm.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one triangle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{slant height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\, \\text{cm} \\times 13 \\, \\text{cm} = 65 \\, \\text{cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the total area of the four triangular faces:**\n\n   Since there are four identical triangular faces:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total area of the four triangles} = 4 \\times 65 \\, \\text{cm}^2 = 260 \\, \\text{cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the total surface area of the pyramid:**\n\n   The total surface area is the sum of the area of the base and the total area of the four triangular faces.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total surface area} = 100 \\, \\text{cm}^2 + 260 \\, \\text{cm}^2 = 360 \\, \\text{cm}^2\n   \\]\n\nThus, the total surface area of the pyramid is \\(\\boxed{360}\\) square centimeters.",
