      "text": "The right pyramid has a square base with side length 10 cm and a height of 12 cm. To find the total surface area of the pyramid, we need to calculate the area of the base and the area of the four triangular faces.\n\nFirst, let's find the area of the base. Since the base is a square with side length 10 cm, the area of the base is:\n\\[\n10 \\times 10 = 100 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the area of one of the triangular faces. The triangular faces form an isosceles triangle with the base being the side of the square base, which is 10 cm, and the height being the distance from the center of the base to the peak of the pyramid, which is 12 cm. To find the length of the base of each triangle, we use the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by half of the base, the height from the center to the peak, and the slant height of the pyramid. The slant height is the hypotenuse of this right triangle.\n\nThe distance from the center of the base to the midpoint of one side of the square (which is half the length of the diagonal of the square) is:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{10^2 - 5^2} = \\sqrt{100 - 25} = \\sqrt{75} = 5\\sqrt{3} \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nSo, the slant height of the pyramid is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs 5 cm and 12 cm:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(5\\sqrt{3})^2 + 12^2} = \\sqrt{75 + 144} = \\sqrt{219} = 3\\sqrt{23} \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nThe area of one triangular face is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 3\\sqrt{23} = 15\\sqrt{23} \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nSince there are four triangular faces, the total area of the four triangular faces is:\n\\[\n4 \\times 15\\sqrt{23} = 60\\sqrt{23} \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nFinally, the total surface area of the pyramid is the sum of the area of the base and the area of the four triangular faces:\n\\[\n100 + 60\\sqrt{23}\n\\]\n\nThus, the total surface area of the pyramid is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{100 + 60\\sqrt{23}}\n\\]",
